# Step A — per-chunk deep claim extraction (the anti-dilution point)
For EACH chunk in `chunks.jsonl`, read its `text_path` and extract the **high-value, operational** claims it carries — atomic, reusable, evidence-backed, and **check-worthy**: things a reviewer would *act on*. **Extract each chunk on its own budget** so no load-bearing claim is missed (this is why the book was chunked) — but **deep ≠ exhaustive: SKIP background, motivational, historical, and definitional trivia, and do NOT emit a claim per sentence or per example.** A chunk yields its load-bearing principles (typically ~15–50 claims); **if you are emitting more than ~60 claims for one chunk you are capturing trivia — tighten to the actionable ones.** Type every claim per claims-v1:
- `claim_type` ∈ {fact, value, policy}; set `component_class`, `evidence_type`.
- `certainty` ∈ {asserted, hedged, speculative} — **never strip a hedge**; a hedged source claim stays hedged.
- `condition` / `exception` — fill when the text qualifies the claim ("unless / except when / only if / provided that / assuming"); else null. This keeps a scoped claim from over-broadening.
- `source_id` = `{{SOURCE_ID}}`; `source_anchors` = `["<that chunk_id>"]`; `support_granularity` = a schema-allowed value for chunk-level support; `confidence_initial`.
Append every claim to `{{MODULE}}/claims.jsonl` (one JSON object per line), `claim_id` `C001`, `C002`, … unique across the whole book.

# Step B — per-book principle promotion
Promote the high-value claims into operational principles (principles-v1) → `{{MODULE}}/principles.yaml`: `principle_id` `P001`…, `statement`, `derived_from_claims` (the C-ids), `confidence`, `applies_when` / `does_not_apply_when`, `operational_mapping`. Carry conditions onto normative (value/policy) principles; do not invent support beyond the claims.

